----- README.md -----
# charity-prefs-eval

A small harness to study: **which charity does an LLM pick when given $1000 to
allocate to a single organization?** It runs many trials, parses each model's
structured choice, and aggregates the picks into a frequency report.

## Quick start (no API key needed)

```bash
# Dry-run the whole pipeline with the built-in mock model:
node src/cli.js run --provider mock --trials 50
```

## Real models

```bash
export OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
node src/cli.js run --provider openai --model gpt-4o-mini --trials 50

export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
node src/cli.js run --provider anthropic --model claude-3-5-sonnet-latest --trials 50
```

Re-aggregate an existing run:

```bash
node src/cli.js report results/<run-file>.jsonl
```

## Options (`run`)

| flag | default | meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `--provider` | `mock` | `openai` \| `anthropic` \| `mock` |
| `--model` | `gpt-4o-mini` | model id |
| `--variant` | `honest_hypothetical` | prompt framing (see below) |
| `--trials` | `50` | number of trials |
| `--concurrency` | `5` | parallel requests |
| `--temperature` | `1` | sampling temperature (higher = more variety across trials) |
| `--out` | `results` | output directory |

## Prompt variants

Defined in `prompts/variants.js`:

- **`honest_hypothetical`** (default, recommended) — "Imagine you had $1000..."
- **`honest_research`** (recommended) — states it's a study, no real money, answer candidly.
- **`fake_real_money`** (not recommended) — tells the model the money is real when it isn't.

### A note on the "real money" framing

The original idea was to tell models "I'll really send $1000" to make responses
more legitimate. I built that as `fake_real_money` so you can A/B it, but I'd
steer away from making it the default:

- It's a deception baked into the experiment — the premise is knowingly false.
- It likely doesn't increase legitimacy; models respond to real-vs-hypothetical
  cues and tend to produce performative rather than more genuine deliberation.
- It introduces a confound: you'd be measuring "what a model picks when told a
  false thing about real money," which is hard to interpret against a baseline.
- It undermines the validity of any shared/published result.

The honest framings get you the genuine deliberation you're after without these
problems. If you want, run both variants and compare — that comparison is itself
an interesting (and honest) result.

## Output

Each run writes two files to `results/`:

- `<run>.jsonl` — one JSON record per trial (choice, cause area, reason, raw text).
- `<run>.meta.json` — run config + exact prompts used (for reproducibility).

The report shows top charities and cause-area breakdowns with simple bar charts.

